{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,220p' docs/reviews/WP-SCP-024/024B-core/r1-safety_bypass.json\"","aggregated_output":"{\"type\":\"result\",\"subtype\":\"success\",\"is_error\":false,\"api_error_status\":null,\"duration_ms\":799599,\"duration_api_ms\":802668,\"num_turns\":22,\"result\":\"I now have all the information needed to produce the review. Let me compile the complete safety_bypass findings for the current code state (post fix-round-10).\\n\\n```json\\n{\\n  \\\"reviewer\\\": \\\"r1-safety_bypass\\\",\\n  \\\"lens\\\": \\\"SAFETY_BYPASS\\\",\\n  \\\"slice\\\": \\\"WP-SCP-024/024B-core\\\",\\n  \\\"date\\\": \\\"2026-05-10\\\",\\n  \\\"round\\\": 4,\\n  \\\"verdict\\\": \\\"CHANGES_REQUESTED\\\",\\n  \\\"findings_count\\\": {\\\"CRIT\\\": 0, \\\"MAJ\\\": 1, \\\"MIN\\\": 4, \\\"nit\\\": 2},\\n  \\\"fixpoint_gate\\\": \\\"0 CRIT + 0 MAJ required; currently 0 CRIT + 1 MAJ\\\",\\n  \\\"review_summary\\\": \\\"Fix-round-10 closed both prior-round blockers. R1-SAFE-CRIT-001 (`git -C $REPO_ROOT` now present at line 248, confirmed by case10c subdirectory test) and SAFE-MAJ-001 (greedy `.+` → `[^—]+`, anchoring to first em-dash and preventing phantom repo on same diff line, confirmed by case4i) are verified fixed. Exactly-one assertions on cascade-status and Target hold. Fail-closed default (exit non-zero for absent/unrecognised), onboarded log+target, onboarded-operator-bump STATUS.md regex, and blocked-on-adopter-conflict negative guard (including truncation case10b and rename case20b) are all correct. Worked-example matrix ex1+ex2 ✅ ex3–ex6 ❌ verified. Template permissions (contents:read + statuses:write only; no id-token/attestations at workflow level), fork-PR refusal clause, adopter-slug canonicalisation (repo.foo_bar → repo-foo-bar via case4h), and bootstrap-only guard position (lines 49–51, before any other processing) are all correct. One MAJ remains: fix-round-10's tooling-slice-id guard rejects cohort IDs 024C–F but a cohort operator can still pass `--tooling-slice-id 024B-core` (or any valid tooling ID), bypassing all enforcement. Workflow-side fix deferred to 024B-extras. Four MINs carry forward: CRLF Target regex, v1.2.0 tag-absent silent skip (overstated in D-044), slug collision false-positive, and post-canonicalisation TOCTOU. Two nits.\\\",\\n  \\\"findings\\\": [\\n    {\\n      \\\"id\\\": \\\"SAFE-MAJ-001\\\",\\n      \\\"severity\\\": \\\"MAJ\\\",\\n      \\\"claim\\\": \\\"Cohort cascade operator (024C–F) can pass `--allow-not-applicable --tooling-slice-id 024B-core` to exit 0 with no log check, no target match, and no TF reference check. Fix-round-10 added tooling-slice-id requirement and cohort-ID rejection but left cross-ID misuse open — no machine barrier prevents a cohort operator supplying a valid tooling ID.\\\",\\n      \\\"evidence\\\": \\\"check-invocation-log-entry.sh lines 179–207: `not applicable` case exits 0 when ALLOW_NOT_APPLICABLE=1 AND TOOLING_SLICE_ID ∈ {024A,024B-core,024B-extras,024G}. Cohort IDs 024C–F are rejected (line 199), but 024B-core is accepted without verifying the invoking slice is actually 024B-core. Code comment (lines 187–195) explicitly acknowledges 'cannot detect operator misuse (e.g. cohort 024C operator passing --tooling-slice-id 024B-core)'. D-044 row describes the gate as 'default refusal for direct CLI use' without acknowledging the residual cross-ID bypass path.\\\",\\n      \\\"impact\\\": \\\"A cohort cascade slice can merge with DISPATCH-NOTE declaring `cascade-status: not applicable`, producing exit 0 and no invocation-log entry, no target verification, and no TF reference. The cascade audit trail for that adopter is entirely absent. Undetectable without manual DISPATCH-NOTE inspection.\\\",\\n      \\\"mitigation\\\": \\\"Within 024B-core scope: add a `slice-type: cohort|tooling` field to DISPATCH-NOTE and validate in the script that `not applicable` only fires for `slice-type: tooling`. Within 024B-extras scope: CI workflow wiring must hardcode `--allow-not-applicable` only for tooling-slice workflow invocations; cohort workflow invocations omit the flag entirely.\\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"id\\\": \\\"SAFE-MIN-001\\\",\\n      \\\"severity\\\": \\\"MIN\\\",\\n      \\\"claim\\\": \\\"Target extraction regex uses `[ \\\\\\\\t]*$` terminal anchor. On CRLF DISPATCH-NOTEs, `\\\\\\\\r` before `\\\\\\\\n` is not matched by `[ \\\\\\\\t]`, causing 0 Target matches → exit 1 for all three enforcement cascade-status paths. The cascade-status extraction (`\\\\\\\\s*$` anchor at line 165) handles CRLF correctly; the Target extraction does not.\\\",\\n      \\\"evidence\\\": \\\"check-invocation-log-entry.sh Target extraction Python block (lines 213–231): `r'^[ \\\\\\\\t]*-[ \\\\\\\\t]+\\\\\\\\*\\\\\\\\*Target:\\\\\\\\*\\\\\\\\*[ \\\\\\\\t]+([a-zA-Z0-9._/-]+)[ \\\\\\\\t]*$'` — `[ \\\\\\\\t]*$` terminal. Compare cascade-status line 165: `r'^cascade-status:\\\\\\\\s+([^\\\\\\\\r\\\\\\\\n]+?)\\\\\\\\s*$'` — `\\\\\\\\s*$` handles `\\\\\\\\r`. No CRLF test case. Windows checkouts or repos with CRLF enabled via git config break all enforcement paths.\\\",\\n      \\\"impact\\\": \\\"On CRLF line-ending DISPATCH-NOTEs, `onboarded`, `onboarded-operator-bump`, and `blocked-on-adopter-conflict` all fail with 'expected exactly one **Target:** match, found 0: []'. Not a silent bypass but breaks cross-platform enforcement reliability.\\\",\\n      \\\"mitigation\\\": \\\"Change `[ \\\\\\\\t]*$` to `\\\\\\\\s*$` in the Target extraction regex. Add a CRLF DISPATCH-NOTE test case (`\\\\\\\\r\\\\\\\\n` line endings) to the CI enforcement test suite.\\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"id\\\": \\\"SAFE-MIN-002\\\",\\n      \\\"severity\\\": \\\"MIN\\\",\\n      \\\"claim\\\": \\\"`git merge-base --is-ancestor v1.2.0 $SCP_SHA 2>/dev/null` silently no-ops when v1.2.0 tag is absent from local clone; TF-023E-002 startup_failure advisory warning is never emitted even for post-v1.2.0 SHAs. D-044 states the warning fires unconditionally, overstating the safety property.\\\",\\n      \\\"evidence\\\": \\\"scaffold-downstream.sh lines 154–158: outer `if [ \\\\\\\"$SCP_SHA\\\\\\\" != \\\\\\\"$V1_0_0_SCP_SHA\\\\\\\" ]` then inner `if git merge-base --is-ancestor v1.2.0 ... 2>/dev/null`. No pre-check for tag existence. `2>/dev/null` swallows 'fatal: Not a valid object name v1.2.0'. Test line 169 gates the warning assertion behind `if git rev-parse v1.2.0 >/dev/null 2>&1` with SKIP path, confirming silent-skip production behaviour. D-044 row: 'emits a TF-023E-002 warning for post-v1.2.0 SHAs' — no conditional qualifier.\\\",\\n      \\\"impact\\\": \\\"Operator using shallow or fresh clone pins post-v1.2.0 SHA without receiving the advisory about attest-scorecard startup_failure. Adopter wrappers silently fail GitHub Actions startup. D-044 trust-model claim is inaccurate for this code path.\\\",\\n      \\\"mitigation\\\": \\\"Before merge-base, check tag existence: `if git -C \\\\\\\"$SCP_REPO_ROOT\\\\\\\" rev-parse v1.2.0 >/dev/null 2>&1; then <merge-base check>; else warn 'v1.2.0 tag absent; TF-023E-002 startup_failure check skipped — verify SHA lineage manually'; fi`. Update D-044 to qualify the warning as conditional on tag presence.\\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"id\\\": \\\"SAFE-MIN-003\\\",\\n      \\\"severity\\\": \\\"MIN\\\",\\n      \\\"claim\\\": \\\"Adopter-slug canonicalization maps `.` and `_` both to `-`, enabling slug collision: `org/repo-foo` and `org/repo.foo` both yield `org-repo-foo`. A pre-existing TF-024X-renovate-org-repo-foo STATUS.md row from adopter A satisfies the onboarded-operator-bump regex check for adopter B's cascade slice without adopter B ever filing its own TF entry.\\\",\\n      \\\"evidence\\\": \\\"canonicalize_adopter_slug (lines 111–117): `re.sub(r'[^a-z0-9-]+', '-', ...)` collapses `.` and `_` with `-`. onboarded-operator-bump STATUS.md pattern (line 305): `rf'TF-024X-renovate-{re.escape(slug)}(?:\\\\\\\\*\\\\\\\\*)? \\\\\\\\((...)'` — re.search against full STATUS.md text. D-044 row acknowledges 'cohort repo names must remain unique after canonicalization' as a precondition but no runtime collision detection exists. Current 5-cohort names do not collide; gap is systemic for future expansions.\\\",\\n      \\\"impact\\\": \\\"A future cohort adopter whose name canonicalizes to an existing slug silently passes the STATUS.md check without a fresh TF row, satisfying the onboarded-operator-bump gate for the wrong adopter and granting false Threshold A credit.\\\",\\n      \\\"mitigation\\\": \\\"Add a startup assertion: grep STATUS.md for `TF-024X-renovate-<slug>` associated with a DIFFERENT adopter repo before proceeding. Document the required uniqueness check in ADOPT-001 §12 per-slice operator checklist.\\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"id\\\": \\\"SAFE-MIN-004\\\",\\n      \\\"severity\\\": \\\"MIN\\\",\\n      \\\"claim\\\": \\\"TOCTOU window between the post-readlink denylist check (lines 143–147) and the first `mkdir -p` + `sed` write (lines 168–179) in scaffold-downstream.sh. A concurrent actor with write access to OUTPUT_DIR's parent can atomically replace the directory with a symlink to a denied system path after the denylist check passes.\\\",\\n      \\\"evidence\\\": \\\"scaffold-downstream.sh: OUTPUT_DIR resolved to a canonical string at lines 134–137; denylist case-pattern check at lines 143–147 on the resolved string; `mkdir -p \\\\\\\"$WORKFLOWS_DIR\\\\\\\"` at line 168; `sed ... >\\\\\\\"$wrapper_path\\\\\\\"` at line 179. No post-mkdir re-verification of resolved path. Condition requires concurrent write access to OUTPUT_DIR parent — low probability on single-operator local machine but non-zero on shared workstations.\\\",\\n      \\\"impact\\\": \\\"If OUTPUT_DIR's parent is world-writable or shared, a race can redirect scaffolder writes into /etc, /var, /usr, or other system paths that the denylist was explicitly designed to block. Low probability, non-trivial consequence.\\\",\\n      \\\"mitigation\\\": \\\"After `mkdir -p` at line 168, verify `python3 os.path.realpath(WORKFLOWS_DIR)` still begins with the expected OUTPUT_DIR prefix; die if it has drifted. Alternatively use Python `os.makedirs` with `follow_symlinks=False` for each directory creation.\\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"id\\\": \\\"SAFE-nit-001\\\",\\n      \\\"severity\\\": \\\"nit\\\",\\n      \\\"claim\\\": \\\"test_check_invocation_log_entry.sh trap uses `trap cleanup EXIT` (line 12) only, omitting INT and TERM. A SIGINT (Ctrl-C) or CI kill-signal leaves TMPDIR in system /tmp including fake-gh executables and scratch git repos.\\\",\\n      \\\"evidence\\\": \\\"test_check_invocation_log_entry.sh line 8: `TMPDIR=$(mktemp -d)` — system /tmp. Line 12: `trap cleanup EXIT`. Contrast test_scaffold_downstream.sh line 15: `trap 'rm -rf \\\\\\\"$TMPDIR\\\\\\\"' EXIT INT TERM`. Accumulated /tmp residue from repeated interrupted runs can cause confusion and leaves executable scripts in shared temp space.\\\",\\n      \\\"impact\\\": \\\"Low severity. Residual fake-gh scripts and scratch git repos in /tmp on interrupt. Inconsistent with the scaffolder test harness pattern and the hygiene goal of clean teardown on any exit path.\\\",\\n      \\\"mitigation\\\": \\\"Change `trap cleanup EXIT` to `trap cleanup EXIT INT TERM` to match the scaffolder test pattern.\\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"id\\\": \\\"SAFE-nit-002\\\",\\n      \\\"severity\\\": \\\"nit\\\",\\n      \\\"claim\\\": \\\"Pre-readlink `..` pattern check in scaffold-downstream.sh (lines 128–132) fires on the RAW path before canonicalisation, rejecting safe paths that contain `..` segments but resolve to non-system locations. The post-readlink denylist (lines 143–147) is the authoritative security boundary and subsumes this protection.\\\",\\n      \\\"evidence\\\": \\\"scaffold-downstream.sh lines 128–132: `case \\\\\\\"$OUTPUT_DIR\\\\\\\" in ..|../*|*/../*|*/..)` fires on raw input. Example: `~/projects/../pim-clone` is rejected even though `readlink -f` would resolve it to a safe non-system path not in the denylist. The denylist at lines 143–147 catches any actually-dangerous resolved path regardless. No security gap; only UX over-restriction.\\\",\\n      \\\"impact\\\": \\\"No security impact. Operators using `..`-containing paths that resolve to legitimate destinations get an unhelpful rejection before canonicalisation. Defense-in-depth value is minimal since the post-canonicalisation denylist already subsumes the protection.\\\",\\n      \\\"mitigation\\\": \\\"No code change required. Optionally add an inline comment clarifying this is a UX guard (reject confusing paths early) not a security boundary, so future maintainers do not remove the post-canonicalisation denylist assuming the early check is sufficient.\\\"\\n    }\\n  ]\\n}\\n```\",\"stop_reason\":\"end_turn\",\"session_id\":\"a0156a7b-c954-42e0-8d82-560adadd4208\",\"total_cost_usd\":1.3012914,\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":15,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":111098,\"cache_read_input_tokens\":755653,\"output_tokens\":43732,\"server_tool_use\":{\"web_search_requests\":0,\"web_fetch_requests\":0},\"service_tier\":\"standard\",\"cache_creation\":{\"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens\":111098,\"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens\":0},\"inference_geo\":\"\",\"iterations\":[{\"input_tokens\":1,\"output_tokens\":17125,\"cache_read_input_tokens\":108500,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":9979,\"cache_creation\":{\"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens\":0,\"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens\":9979},\"type\":\"message\"}],\"speed\":\"standard\"},\"modelUsage\":{\"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001\":{\"inputTokens\":1838,\"outputTokens\":23,\"cacheReadInputTokens\":0,\"cacheCreationInputTokens\":0,\"webSearchRequests\":0,\"costUSD\":0.001953,\"contextWindow\":200000,\"maxOutputTokens\":32000},\"claude-sonnet-4-6\":{\"inputTokens\":15,\"outputTokens\":43732,\"cacheReadInputTokens\":755653,\"cacheCreationInputTokens\":111098,\"webSearchRequests\":0,\"costUSD\":1.2993384000000001,\"contextWindow\":200000,\"maxOutputTokens\":32000}},\"permission_denials\":[],\"terminal_reason\":\"completed\",\"fast_mode_state\":\"off\",\"uuid\":\"2696d045-912b-4060-baf4-52398c37533d\"}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
